---
import DocLayout from "../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import Playground from "../components/Playground.astro";
---

<DocLayout
  title="Playground"
  description="Edit a tour's JSON configuration and watch the real @take-a-tour/core library render it."
>
  <h1>Playground</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This runs the actual <code>@take-a-tour/core</code> package — the same code published to npm,
    imported by name. There is no mock and no parallel implementation.
  </p>

  <Playground />

  <h2>What to try</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Change <code>"placement"</code> to <code>"left"</code>, <code>"right"</code> or
      <code>"top"</code> and restart.
    </li>
    <li>Switch <code>"theme"</code> between <code>light</code>, <code>dark</code> and <code>system</code>.</li>
    <li>
      Remove a step's <code>tourId</code> and press Start to read the validation error the library
      actually throws.
    </li>
    <li>
      Add a second element with the same <code>data-tour-id</code> in the console to see the
      ambiguity report.
    </li>
    <li>Open the <strong>CSS variables</strong> tab and uncomment <code>--tat-tooltip-width</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Starting code</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="json"
    code={`{
  "tourId": "playground",
  "theme": "system",
  "placement": "bottom",
  "showProgress": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "tourId": "play.nav",
      "title": "Start here",
      "content": "This panel is a live preview. Press Next to move on.",
      "placement": "right"
    }
  ]
}`}
  />

  <Callout title="The preview surface is real DOM">
    <p>
      The three elements in the preview carry real <code>data-tour-id</code> attributes, so
      target resolution, spotlight geometry and repositioning all behave exactly as they would in
      your application.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</DocLayout>